{"lines":[{"t":"Reading","x":0.80055456495274635,"y":0.05573482345490921,"h":0.016071952087834629,"c":1,"w":0.07976259867350266},{"y":0.84738372115877503,"h":0.015988371407532842,"c":1,"w":0.018796992301940922,"t":"D","x":0.062030075197042435},{"t":"Why being bored is stimulating - and useful, too","x":0.15198574577473853,"y":0.098656171859082042,"c":1,"w":0.64901199340820315,"h":0.031218226003554728},{"w":0.7803445434570313,"t":"This most common of emotions is turning out to be more interesting than","x":0.086322864574773159,"y":0.14410729766057195,"h":0.024843610185081522,"c":1},{"h":0.019712842362816008,"w":0.12238810221354168,"y":0.17110288112305982,"c":1,"x":0.41530970823808633,"t":"we thought"},{"t":"We all know how it feels - it's impossible to keep your mind on anything, time","w":0.70149510701497397,"h":0.023723901968499717,"c":1,"x":0.11635500535916975,"y":0.19763645700258581},{"x":0.11453907340636751,"y":0.21735996837135252,"t":"stretches out, and all the things you could do seem equally unlikely to make you","w":0.72957753499348965,"c":1,"h":0.022159957148840004},{"h":0.021673761332180908,"t":"feel better. But defining boredom so that it can be studied in the lab has proved","y":0.23685968261183832,"c":1,"x":0.11266360419608816,"w":0.7220306396484375},{"x":0.11447068368969525,"y":0.25469142907973508,"w":0.73913619995117175,"t":"difficult. For a start, it can include a lot of other mental states, such as frustration,","h":0.0244511016629545,"c":1},{"y":0.2761627905646955,"x":0.11466164307125003,"t":"apathy, depression and indifference. There isn't even agreement over whether","w":0.71428573608398438,"h":0.017462450998948609,"c":1},{"x":0.11456128568523184,"y":0.29344575993330624,"w":0.72762339274088539,"h":0.020878503803123061,"c":1,"t":"boredom is always a low-energy, flat kind of emotion or whether feeling agitated"},{"h":0.021698715298389182,"c":1,"t":"and restless counts as boredom, too. In his book, Boredom: A Lively History, Peter","x":0.11454926162986036,"y":0.31182068475553359,"w":0.75395090738932291},{"c":1,"t":"Toohey at the University of Calgary, Canada, compares it to disgust - an emotion","h":0.022523748590649872,"w":0.73899546305338537,"x":0.11451196022215204,"y":0.33102454475031873},{"c":1,"x":0.11257500410823183,"t":"that motivates us to stay away from certain situations. 'If disgust protects humans","w":0.74287022908528644,"h":0.025003594117861683,"y":0.34735964224842664},{"h":0.020632130364333867,"c":1,"t":"from infection, boredom may protect them from 'infectious® social situations,'","x":0.11271176598349295,"y":0.36893924467641381,"w":0.69936564127604162},{"y":0.38897351683363945,"h":0.017111105835982743,"c":1,"t":"he suggests.","x":0.11266108031249715,"w":0.12054250081380209},{"y":0.41689144724652094,"x":0.10141661311538561,"w":0.7727111307779948,"c":1,"h":0.021517636310800947,"t":"By asking people about their experiences of boredom, Thomas Goetz and his team"},{"w":0.76161961873372386,"t":"at the University of Konstanz in Germany have recently identified five distinct types:","c":1,"y":0.43402591728124773,"x":0.11262939983088747,"h":0.022895277505986411},{"t":"indifferent, calibrating, searching, reactant and apathetic. These can be plotted on","c":1,"h":0.023518389758953151,"x":0.10887220775072023,"y":0.4531896158441705,"w":0.74842458089192709},{"h":0.022391292869238799,"x":0.11076286414001714,"y":0.47230812215581586,"t":"two axes - one running left to right, which measures low to high arousal, and the","c":1,"w":0.73523661295572917},{"y":0.49113791095906545,"t":"other from top to bottom, which measures how positive or negative the feeling is.","x":0.11084877660748096,"c":1,"h":0.020581344290080628,"w":0.73883967081705737},{"c":1,"y":0.50988372447777686,"t":"Intriguingly, Goetz has found that while people experience all kinds of boredom,","h":0.020665957861689677,"w":0.72759109497070307,"x":0.11082906575750494},{"h":0.022063856499469958,"x":0.11076773772849019,"c":1,"y":0.52797380312921027,"w":0.7257630411783853,"t":"they tend to specialise in one. Of the five types, the most damaging is 'reactant'"},{"c":1,"x":0.1107747902810876,"w":0.75024449666341153,"t":"boredom with its explosive combination of high arousal and negative emotion. The","h":0.021033715065724601,"y":0.5475300724274681},{"y":0.56641750661841961,"c":1,"t":"most useful is what Goetz calls 'indifferent' boredom: someone isn't engaged in","x":0.11079988298462438,"w":0.72200978597005205,"h":0.021163854457759368},{"w":0.74653315226236971,"h":0.023070706909729899,"y":0.5842910647374161,"c":1,"x":0.11075703188611537,"t":"anything satisfying but still feels relaxed and calm. However, it remains to be seen"},{"t":"whether there are any character traits that predict the kind of boredom each of us","h":0.019422662542162517,"c":1,"w":0.74263244628906255,"x":0.10894652151646046,"y":0.60446723187465012},{"t":"might be prone to.","y":0.62202189293104182,"w":0.17105262756347658,"h":0.018995492594977459,"x":0.10902255668348734,"c":1},{"h":0.023733351511565304,"c":1,"x":0.095694397355094343,"y":0.65042018503155119,"t":"Psychologist Sandi Mann at the University of Central Lancashire, UK, goes further.","w":0.77102879842122396},{"w":0.75781865437825524,"x":0.10886094672291145,"h":0.022820543337083166,"t":"'All emotions are there for a reason, including boredom,' she says. Mann has found","c":1,"y":0.66860472751045374},{"c":1,"t":"that being bored makes us more creative. 'We're all afraid of being bored but in","y":0.68798823719975288,"x":0.10700967117834106,"w":0.72395284016927086,"h":0.022035334698246589},{"w":0.70904362996419268,"x":0.10882477072590803,"t":"actual fact it can lead to all kinds of amazing things,' she says. In experiments","y":0.7046884466272253,"h":0.025125299663752432,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.022812017378159188,"t":"published last year, Mann found that people who had been made to feel bored by","x":0.10887885960511728,"y":0.72646897701803903,"w":0.74089950561523432},{"w":0.75765482584635424,"t":"copying numbers out of the phone book for 15 minutes came up with more creative","y":0.74528155038120958,"h":0.019782313821550712,"c":1,"x":0.10895412928998742},{"x":0.10700782833032614,"w":0.73333419799804689,"t":"ideas about how to use a polystyrene cup than a control group. Mann concluded","y":0.7644500036899432,"c":1,"h":0.021273560778832246},{"x":0.10714285732742089,"w":0.697368418375651,"y":0.78332260166806544,"t":"that a passive, boring activity is best for creativity because it allows the mind","h":0.017707662588538287,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.021591908687633388,"y":0.8015410217320974,"t":"to wander. In fact, she goes so far as to suggest that we should seek out more","x":0.10512826189312259,"w":0.7164384460449218},{"x":0.10696172003003578,"y":0.81887102835038605,"w":0.1959332021077474,"t":"boredom in our lives.","h":0.01844592318562488,"c":1},{"y":0.84966340121369277,"c":1,"w":0.76897176106770837,"x":0.1014229748059384,"h":0.020665539055582882,"t":"Psychologist John Eastwood at York University in Toronto, Canada, isn't convinced."},{"w":0.73155426025390624,"t":"'If you are in a state of mind-wandering you are not bored,' he says. 'In my view,","y":0.86727821982291942,"x":0.1050971017156983,"h":0.022327178689793281,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.10516118612689468,"w":0.70698374430338551,"h":0.021213607640705567,"t":"by definition boredom is an undesirable state.' That doesn't necessarily mean","y":0.88609674963363627},{"w":0.72775497436523429,"y":0.90519643211636536,"x":0.10511522423393634,"c":1,"h":0.022179440844081966,"t":"that it isn't adaptive, he adds. 'Pain is adaptive - if we didn't have physical pain,"},{"y":0.92404334388619602,"w":0.71267232259114588,"h":0.021453324395920959,"c":1,"t":"bad things would happen to us. Does that mean that we should actively cause","x":0.1051283112737742},{"w":0.73518112182617179,"x":0.10703166378944691,"h":0.02289316014021503,"c":1,"t":"pain? No. But even if boredom has evolved to help us survive, it can still be toxic","y":0.94301424641247977},{"h":0.014534883321060788,"x":0.84398496212430019,"y":0.97529069772900445,"w":0.028195489247639927,"c":1,"t":"21"}],"h":3106,"w":2400}